or open water, sea, ice or town the profile does not name as habitat. None of these is a finding that the species is absent.","Land cover is from 2019 and does not know forest age, recent fire or harvest.","Drawn from records made in September to February, so it shows where the species is in the hunting season, not where it breeds.","Hunting geography and seasons play no part in this surface; the zone card says what is legal."],"observationPeriod":{"from":"2000-01-01","through":"2026-09-30"},"methodology":{"id":"methodology:north-ground-range-habitat","version":"2.1.0","effectiveFrom":"2026-09-30","history":[{"version":"1.0.0","detail":"First publication: range from clustered record squares, categorical habitat inside it."},{"version":"2.0.0","detail":"Ground inside the range rated unsuitable kept as its own state; water, sea, ice and town masked for profiles that do not name them; values placed on the grid nodes the renderer samples (1.0.0 sat half a cell south-west); an island rule for sedentary families; elevation and coast requirements; confidence from components rather than record counts alone. The concentrated-population rule (2 counted squares holding 300 records) was set AFTER the first 2.0.0 build declined kalij pheasant on 15,421 records in three squares of Hawaii Island: the five-square minimum was meant to refuse a handful of scattered reports, and refused a real island population instead. Recorded as set after seeing the result."},{"version":"2.1.0","detail":"Open water a profile names is drawn only within the profile's openWaterKm (else coastKm, else 5 km) of dry land, as the sea already was: under 2.0.0 mallard was painted across the middle of Lake Winnipeg. 'Water or wetland nearby' is a distance-weighted share falling to nothing at 20 km, replacing an unweighted 3 × 3 box that drew a hard-edged 0.3° square around every small lake (wild boar). Both set after seeing those surfaces. Gaps in the range no wider than the family's declared gapKm are joined by a morphological closing (grow by half the gap, shrink back), which can fill a hole between recorded places but never extend the range past its recorded edge or outside its convex hull; habitat and the masks still decide every joined cell. Set AFTER seeing the 2.0.0 alligator surface: 9,702 openly licensed records in 72 squares drew separate discs with central Florida blank — the records' sampling painted as absence. Recorded as set after seeing the result. For a family with an island rule, a population on a separate landmass needs that many records on it: set after kalij pheasant was drawn on Kauaʻi from 3 records, carried across the channel by the 150 km cluster rule. A cell is confirmed by 2 records in it and the eight cells around it, not in it alone: set after the 0.35° reads drew moose without the boreal core and red fox in fragments, because half of every species' record cells hold a single record, most with a record next door. And a correction, not a choice: each record square is now used at the size of the GBIF cell it stands for. The first reads' 0.35° squares were 1.40625° cells (GBIF aggregates 16 × 16 cells per map tile), so 2.0.0 drew ranges from a sixteenth of the ground the records covered, in bands with gaps between them."}]},"methodologyStatedAs":"Range: ground within 40 km of GBIF's 0.3515625° record cells with 2+ openly licensed records since 2000 in them and the eight cells around them, each within 150 km of another, with gaps of up to 60 km between recorded ground joined (5699 squares, 1637841 records, September to February). Habitat: each 0.1° cell's land cover read through this species' categorical profile (core 1, high 0.75, moderate 0.5, low 0.25, unsuitable 0), share-weighted; open water, sea, ice and town the profile does not name are masked. No weight is fitted.","scaleStatedAs":"Ruddy duck: habitat opportunity. Colour is the habitat class for this species inside its range — red core, orange and yellow strong, green moderate, blue marginal. Habitat, not a count of animals.","season":{"observedSeason":"September to February records (the hunting season)","matchesHuntingSeason":true,"warning":"Records from September to February only, so this is the species' hunting-season distribution, not its breeding range."},"model":{"id":"model:north-ground-range-habitat-ruddy-duck","version":"2.1.0","claim":"RANGE_HABITAT","inputs":[{"id":"foundation:landcover-cgls-lc100-2019-0.1deg","hash":"sha256:37891209b4ae816931a1f6a944c5ac2d2403c00f4cbfda71cacd710a050de525"},{"id":"open occurrence records (content/intelligence/range-habitat/inputs/ruddy-duck.records.json)","hash":"sha256:5c0fef639c37777d3cbcaf4a8e20bea0d42f5a5591967e1f409bfdca05681397"}],"literature":["Breeds on fresh or alkaline lakes and ponds with marshy borders and open water; winters on sheltered shallow coastal bays and ice-free inland lakes. — North Ground's published profile of the species, citing National Audubon Society, Ruddy Duck | Audubon Field Guide; NatureServe, Oxyura jamaicensis (NatureServe Explorer)."],"reading":"In the hunting season: sheltered shallow coastal bays (sea within 10 km of land) and ice-free inland lakes are core; marsh marginal.","profile":{"family":"WATERFOWL_SEASON","reachKm":40,"landCover":{"CORE":["WATER","SEA"],"LOW":["WETLAND"]},"edge":null,"requires":[],"coastKm":10,"season":"HUNTING_SEASON_RECORDS","whyNotRangeHabitat":null},"range":{"confirmedSquares":5710,"countedSquares":5699,"islandSquares":0,"landmassesWithTooFewRecords":0,"gapKm":60,"joinedCells":1313,"recordsNotPlaced":0,"recordGroup":"BIRD","edgeOnUnrecordedGround":0.042,"openRecords":1637841,"datasets":31,"months":[[9,12],[1,2]]},"cells":{"painted":7703,"unsuitable":110446,"masked":{"SNOW_ICE":99,"BUILT":595,"WATER":1286},"noData":0},"variation":{"classShares":{"LOW":0.269,"MODERATE":0.183,"CORE":0.394,"HIGH":0.154},"usefulVariation":true},"confidenceComponents":[{"component":"range evidence","holds":true,"detail":"1637841 records in 5699 squares (needs 